    body: `
<p>English says "the kitchen" and "my phone" with little words that never change. Portuguese chooses the little word to match the thing, and it keeps that match in front of "my" and "your". This lesson is the home: the rooms, what you look for in them, and where it is.</p>
<h3>The rooms, and the word in front</h3>
<p>Most rooms take <b>a</b>. <b>A casa</b> is the house. <b>A cozinha</b> is the kitchen. <b>A sala</b> is the living room, and <b>a sala de estar</b> is the longer name. <b>A casa de banho</b> is the bathroom, the whole phrase on a door in Portugal.</p>
<p>A few everyday things take <b>o</b>. <b>O quarto</b> is the bedroom. <b>O telemóvel</b> is the cell phone. <b>O livro</b> is a book, <b>o sofá</b> the sofa, <b>o frigorífico</b> the fridge. <b>A chave</b> is a key and <b>a cama</b> is a bed. Both take <b>a</b>.</p>
{{ex:A minha casa é pequena.|uh MEE-nyuh KAH-zuh eh pih-KAY-nuh|My house is small.}}
{{ex:O frigorífico está na cozinha.|oo free-goo-REE-fee-koo ihsh-TAH nuh koo-ZEE-nyuh|The fridge is in the kitchen.}}
<p>"My" and "your" keep the same match, article first: <b>o meu telemóvel</b>, <b>a minha casa</b>, <b>a tua chave</b>. <b>Pequena</b> matches <b>casa</b>. Portugal also usually puts <b>a</b> or <b>o</b> before a first name: <b>a Ana</b>, <b>o João</b>.</p>
<h3>In the room, or on the way there</h3>
<p>English keeps "in" and "the" apart. Portuguese joins them. A feminine place takes <b>na</b>: <b>na cozinha</b>, <b>na sala</b>, <b>na cama</b>. On the bed is that same <b>na</b>. A masculine place takes <b>no</b>: <b>no quarto</b>, <b>no sofá</b>. The verb is <b>está</b>. Two or more things take <b>estão</b>.</p>
{{ex:O telemóvel está na cama.|oo teh-leh-MAW-vehl ihsh-TAH nuh KUH-muh|The phone is on the bed.}}
{{ex:A chave está na cozinha.|uh SHAH-vih ihsh-TAH nuh koo-ZEE-nyuh|The key is in the kitchen.}}
{{ex:O livro está no quarto.|oo LEE-vroo ihsh-TAH noo KWAHR-too|The book is in the bedroom.}}
{{ex:O meu livro está no sofá.|oo mayw LEE-vroo ihsh-TAH noo soo-FAH|My book is on the sofa.}}
{{ex:As chaves estão na cozinha.|uhsh SHAH-vihsh ihsh-TOWNG nuh koo-ZEE-nyuh|The keys are in the kitchen.}}
<p>Going there joins "to" and "the". <b>A</b> plus <b>a</b> is <b>à</b>, with the accent: <b>vou à cozinha</b>, <b>vou à casa de banho</b>. <b>A</b> plus <b>o</b> is <b>ao</b>: <b>vou ao quarto</b>. <b>Na</b> means you are already there. <b>À</b> means you are heading there.</p>
{{ex:Vou à casa de banho.|voh ah KAH-zuh dih BUH-nyoo|I'm going to the bathroom.}}
<h3>Right now, in that room</h3>
<p>Someone in the middle of a job uses <b>estar a</b> plus the verb unchanged. <b>A Ana está a trabalhar na sala</b>. In Brazil the <b>-ndo</b> form is the everyday wording. In Portugal this sentence stays <b>estar a</b>.</p>
{{ex:A Ana está a trabalhar na sala.|uh UH-nuh ihsh-TAH uh truh-buh-LYAHR nuh SAH-luh|Ana is working in the living room.}}
<div class="tip">The slip to fix first: <b>O cozinha está em a casa</b> → <b>A cozinha está na casa</b>. The word in front matches the room, and "in the" is already one word.</div>
`,
