    title: "Around town",
    blurb: "Ask where a place in town is, and say it is near, next to, or in front of something.",
    deck: "Around town and directions",
    body: `
<p>You already ask <b>onde está</b> for a phone, a book, a person: something that moves. A building stays put. For the pharmacy, the station, the market, the usual question is <b>onde fica</b>. <b>Fica</b> is one place. Two places take <b>ficam</b>. The rest of the sentence is a small map: near, next to, or in front of.</p>
<h3>Three ways to place it</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Portuguese</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">perto de</td><td>near</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ao lado de</td><td>next to</td></tr><tr><td class="k">em frente de</td><td>in front of</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Each one ends in <b>de</b>, and <b>de</b> still joins the article, the same way it does with <b>gostar</b>. A masculine place gives <b>do</b>. A feminine place gives <b>da</b>. <b>Perto do metro</b>. <b>Ao lado da estação</b>. <b>Em frente do parque</b>. The <b>ao</b> in <b>ao lado</b> is already part of that phrase. It means "at the side", and then <b>de</b> names the neighbor.</p>
{{ex:Onde fica a farmácia?|OHN-dih FEE-kuh uh fuhr-MAH-syuh|Where is the pharmacy?}}
{{ex:A farmácia fica perto do metro.|uh fuhr-MAH-syuh FEE-kuh PEHR-too doo MEH-troo|The pharmacy is near the metro.}}
{{ex:O mercado fica ao lado da estação.|oo mihr-KAH-doo FEE-kuh ow LAH-doo duh ihsh-tuh-SOWNG|The market is next to the station.}}
{{ex:A praia fica em frente do parque.|uh PRAHY-uh FEE-kuh uhyng FRAYN-tih doo PAHR-kih|The beach is in front of the park.}}
<h3>The places, and which little word</h3>
<p><b>A farmácia</b> is the pharmacy. <b>A estação</b> is the station. <b>A praia</b> is the beach. <b>A rua</b> is the street. Those four take <b>a</b>. <b>O mercado</b> is the market. <b>O parque</b> is the park. <b>O banco</b> is the bank. <b>O metro</b> is the metro. Those four take <b>o</b>. On the street is <b>na rua</b>, because <b>em</b> plus <b>a</b> is <b>na</b>. Next to the bank is <b>ao lado do banco</b>.</p>
{{ex:O banco fica na rua.|oo BUHN-koo FEE-kuh nuh KHOO-uh|The bank is on the street.}}
{{ex:O parque fica ao lado do banco.|oo PAHR-kih FEE-kuh ow LAH-doo doo BUHN-koo|The park is next to the bank.}}
<p>Lisbon and Porto keep the split you have already seen. Lisbon takes no article in these lines, so "near Lisbon" is <b>perto de Lisboa</b> and "in Lisbon" stays <b>em Lisboa</b>. Porto takes <b>o</b>, so "in Porto" is <b>no Porto</b> and "near Porto" is <b>perto do Porto</b>. One place, one <b>fica</b>. The beach and the park together take <b>ficam</b>.</p>
{{ex:A praia fica perto de Lisboa.|uh PRAHY-uh FEE-kuh PEHR-too dih lihzh-BOH-uh|The beach is near Lisbon.}}
{{ex:A estação fica no Porto.|uh ihsh-tuh-SOWNG FEE-kuh noo POHR-too|The station is in Porto.}}
{{ex:A praia e o parque ficam perto do banco.|uh PRAHY-uh ee oo PAHR-kih FEE-kowng PEHR-too doo BUHN-koo|The beach and the park are near the bank.}}
<div class="tip">The slip to fix first: <b>perto de o metro</b> → <b>perto do metro</b>. <b>De</b> and <b>o</b> are already one word, and the metro is masculine, so the word is <b>do</b>.</div>
`,
