    id: "transport",
    title: "Transport",
    blurb: "Say you are going by bus, train or metro, and name the ticket you need.",
    deck: "Travel and transport",
    body: `
<p>The three rides you will actually take have Portugal words, and the English word will not get you the ticket. <b>O autocarro</b> is the bus. <b>O comboio</b> is the train. <b>O metro</b> is the metro. All three are masculine, so the word in front is <b>o</b>. In Brazil the bus and the train have other names. On a sign in Portugal you want these three.</p>
<h3>By the bus, or on the bus</h3>
<p>"By" is <b>de</b>, and here <b>de</b> does not join the next word. <b>De autocarro</b>, <b>de comboio</b>, <b>de metro</b>. The destination after that is <b>para</b>. Lisbon takes no article, so the line is <b>para Lisboa</b>. Porto takes <b>o</b>, so the line is <b>para o Porto</b>. <b>Para</b> does not swallow <b>o</b> the way <b>a</b> does.</p>
{{ex:Vou de autocarro para Lisboa.|voh dih ow-taw-KAH-khoo PUH-ruh lihzh-BOH-uh|I'm going by bus to Lisbon.}}
{{ex:Vou de comboio para o Porto.|voh dih kohm-BAWY-oo PUH-ruh oo POHR-too|I'm going by train to Porto.}}
{{ex:Vou de metro para a estação.|voh dih MEH-troo PUH-ruh uh ihsh-tuh-SOWNG|I'm going by metro to the station.}}
{{ex:Amanhã vou de comboio para Lisboa.|ah-muh-NYUHNG voh dih kohm-BAWY-oo PUH-ruh lihzh-BOH-uh|Tomorrow I'm going by train to Lisbon.}}
<p>On the vehicle, as a place, you are back to <b>estar</b> and <b>em</b>, and <b>em</b> does join. <b>Estou no autocarro</b>. <b>Estou no comboio</b>. <b>Estou no metro</b>. <b>No</b> is "on" or "in". <b>De</b> is "by". <b>Vou de comboio</b> means you travel by train. <b>Estou no comboio</b> means you are on it now. The station, <b>a estação</b>, is feminine, so "to the station" in this <b>para</b> line is <b>para a estação</b>, with a plain <b>a</b> and no accent. "At the station" is <b>na estação</b>.</p>
{{ex:Estou no autocarro.|ihsh-TOH noo ow-taw-KAH-khoo|I'm on the bus.}}
{{ex:Estou no comboio.|ihsh-TOH noo kohm-BAWY-oo|I'm on the train.}}
{{ex:Quero ir de autocarro.|KEH-roo eer dih ow-taw-KAH-khoo|I want to go by bus.}}
<h3>The ticket</h3>
<p>A ticket is <b>um bilhete</b>. The word is masculine, so <b>um</b>, never <b>uma</b>. The destination uses <b>para</b> again. <b>Um bilhete para Lisboa</b>. <b>Um bilhete para o Porto</b>. At the window, the soft request you already use in a café still works: <b>Queria um bilhete para Lisboa, se faz favor</b>. One ticket, one city, and Lisbon still takes no article after <b>para</b>.</p>
<div class="tip">The slip to fix first: <b>Vou em autocarro para Lisboa</b> → <b>Vou de autocarro para Lisboa</b>. <b>De</b> is by. <b>Em</b> is for being on it: <b>estou no autocarro</b>.</div>
`,
