    id: "plans",
    title: "Plans and obligations",
    blurb: "Say what you are going to do, what you have to do, and what you can do.",
    deck: "Plans and obligations",
    body: `
<p>English talks about a plan with "going to": "I'm going to visit Porto." Portuguese uses <b>vou</b> plus the verb, and nothing sits between them. <b>Vou visitar o Porto</b>. The little <b>a</b> that English and Spanish both reach for belongs to a place, not to the next verb. <b>Vou a Lisboa</b> is a place. <b>Vou visitar Lisboa</b> is a plan. Put <b>a</b> in the plan and you have said it twice.</p>
<h3>A place, and a plan</h3>
<p>Lisbon takes no article, so the place line is <b>Vou a Lisboa</b>, with a plain <b>a</b> and no accent. Porto takes <b>o</b>, so <b>a</b> plus <b>o</b> is <b>ao</b>: <b>Vou ao Porto</b>. The plan keeps the article on the city and drops the <b>a</b> before the verb: <b>Vou visitar o Porto</b>. <b>Não</b> still sits in front of <b>vou</b>. Tomorrow is <b>amanhã</b>, and it can open the sentence: <b>Amanhã vou a Lisboa</b>.</p>
{{ex:Vou visitar o Porto.|voh vee-zee-TAHR oo POHR-too|I'm going to visit Porto.}}
{{ex:Vou a Lisboa.|voh uh lihzh-BOH-uh|I'm going to Lisbon.}}
{{ex:Vou ao Porto.|voh ow POHR-too|I'm going to Porto.}}
{{ex:Amanhã vou a Lisboa.|ah-muh-NYUHNG voh uh lihzh-BOH-uh|Tomorrow I'm going to Lisbon.}}
{{ex:Quero ir à praia.|KEH-roo eer ah PRAHY-uh|I want to go to the beach.}}
<h3>Have to, can, should</h3>
<p><b>Ter de</b> plus the unchanged verb is "have to". <b>Tenho de trabalhar</b>. <b>Tenho de ir ao Porto</b>. The <b>de</b> does not become <b>do</b> here, because an infinitive has no article. You will also hear <b>tenho que</b>. This lesson practices <b>de</b>. <b>Não tenho de trabalhar</b> is "I don't have to".</p>
{{ex:Tenho de trabalhar.|TUH-nyoo dih truh-buh-LYAHR|I have to work.}}
{{ex:Tenho de ir ao Porto.|TUH-nyoo dih eer ow POHR-too|I have to go to Porto.}}
{{ex:Não tenho de trabalhar.|nowng TUH-nyoo dih truh-buh-LYAHR|I don't have to work.}}
<p><b>Posso</b> plus the verb is "I can". <b>Posso ir a Lisboa</b>. <b>O senhor</b> and <b>a senhora</b> take <b>pode</b>, the <b>ele</b> form: <b>O senhor pode ir amanhã</b>. <b>Devo</b> plus the verb is "I should" or "I'm supposed to". It is the same shape as <b>tenho de</b> and <b>posso</b>: the person changes, and the next verb stays whole. <b>Devo ir a Lisboa</b>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>should</th></tr><tr><td>eu</td><td class="k">devo</td></tr><tr><td>tu</td><td class="k">deves</td></tr><tr><td>ele, ela, o senhor, a senhora</td><td class="k">deve</td></tr><tr><td>nós</td><td class="k">devemos</td></tr><tr><td>vocês, eles, elas</td><td class="k">devem</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Posso ir a Lisboa.|PAW-soo eer uh lihzh-BOH-uh|I can go to Lisbon.}}
{{ex:O senhor pode ir amanhã.|oo sih-NYOHR PAW-dih eer ah-muh-NYUHNG|The gentleman can go tomorrow.}}
<div class="tip">The slip to fix first: <b>Vou a visitar o Porto</b> → <b>Vou visitar o Porto</b>. The <b>a</b> is for a place, as in <b>vou ao Porto</b>. A plan puts the verb straight after <b>vou</b>.</div>
`,
