    id: "past",
    title: "The past: pretérito perfeito",
    blurb: "Say what you did, where you went, and what you liked, as a finished event.",
    deck: "Likes and free time",
    body: `
<p>For a finished event, English often uses two words. Portuguese uses one: <b>falei</b>. This is the simple past, the <b>pretérito perfeito</b>: yesterday, this morning once it is over, a trip you already took.</p>
<h3>Regular verbs, and the nós accent</h3>
<p>An <b>-ar</b> verb changes the ending, and the <b>nós</b> line is the one to watch. Portugal writes an accent on it, and the present does not. Present <b>falamos</b> is "we speak". Past <b>falámos</b> is "we spoke". In the aid, the present vowel is UH and the past vowel is AH. <b>Ontem falámos no Porto</b>. <b>Hoje falamos</b>. The <b>tu</b> form of that past is <b>falaste</b>. <b>O senhor</b> takes <b>falou</b>, the <b>ele</b> form.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>falar, past</th></tr><tr><td>eu</td><td class="k">falei</td></tr><tr><td>tu</td><td class="k">falaste</td></tr><tr><td>ele, ela, o senhor, a senhora</td><td class="k">falou</td></tr><tr><td>nós</td><td class="k">falámos</td></tr><tr><td>vocês, eles, elas</td><td class="k">falaram</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Ontem falei com a Ana.|OHN-tuhyng fuh-LUHY kohng uh UH-nuh|Yesterday I spoke with Ana.}}
{{ex:Hoje falamos.|OH-zhih fuh-LUH-moosh|Today we speak.}}
{{ex:Ontem falámos no Porto.|OHN-tuhyng fuh-LAH-moosh noo POHR-too|Yesterday we spoke in Porto.}}
{{ex:Falaste com a Maria?|fuh-LAHSH-tih kohng uh muh-REE-uh|Did you speak with Maria?}}
<p>For <b>-er</b> and <b>-ir</b>, the <b>nós</b> form is spelled the same in the past and the present. <b>Ontem</b> marks the past. <b>Gostar</b> is <b>-ar</b>, so the <b>nós</b> past keeps the accent: <b>gostámos</b>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>comer</th><th>partir</th></tr><tr><td>eu</td><td class="k">comi</td><td class="k">parti</td></tr><tr><td>tu</td><td class="k">comeste</td><td class="k">partiste</td></tr><tr><td>ele</td><td class="k">comeu</td><td class="k">partiu</td></tr><tr><td>nós</td><td class="k">comemos</td><td class="k">partimos</td></tr><tr><td>eles</td><td class="k">comeram</td><td class="k">partiram</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Gostei de Lisboa.|goosh-TUHY dih lihzh-BOH-uh|I liked Lisbon.}}
{{ex:A Ana gostou do queijo.|uh UH-nuh goosh-TOH doo KUHY-zhoo|Ana liked the cheese.}}
{{ex:Ontem comi o pastel.|OHN-tuhyng koo-MEE oo puhsh-TEHL|Yesterday I ate the tart.}}
{{ex:O comboio partiu.|oo kohm-BAWY-oo puhr-TEEW|The train left.}}
<h3>ser, estar, ir, fazer</h3>
<p><b>Ser</b> and <b>ir</b> share one past, and the rest of the sentence tells them apart. <b>Fui a Lisboa</b> is "I went". <b>O almoço foi bom</b> is "the lunch was good", and <b>bom</b> matches masculine <b>almoço</b>. A place or a state uses <b>estar</b>: <b>estive em Lisboa</b>, <b>estive cansada</b> for a woman, <b>estive cansado</b> for a man. <b>Fazer</b> starts with <b>fiz</b>. Went is <b>fui a</b>. Was there is <b>estive em</b>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>ser, ir</th><th>estar</th><th>fazer</th></tr><tr><td>eu</td><td class="k">fui</td><td class="k">estive</td><td class="k">fiz</td></tr><tr><td>tu</td><td class="k">foste</td><td class="k">estiveste</td><td class="k">fizeste</td></tr><tr><td>ele</td><td class="k">foi</td><td class="k">esteve</td><td class="k">fez</td></tr><tr><td>nós</td><td class="k">fomos</td><td class="k">estivemos</td><td class="k">fizemos</td></tr><tr><td>eles</td><td class="k">foram</td><td class="k">estiveram</td><td class="k">fizeram</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Ontem fui a Lisboa.|OHN-tuhyng fooy uh lihzh-BOH-uh|Yesterday I went to Lisbon.}}
{{ex:Estive em Lisboa.|ihsh-TEE-vih uhyng lihzh-BOH-uh|I was in Lisbon.}}
{{ex:O almoço foi bom.|oo ahl-MOH-soo foy bohng|The lunch was good.}}
{{ex:Estive cansada.|ihsh-TEE-vih kuhn-SAH-duh|I was tired. (said by a woman)}}
{{ex:Ontem fiz o pequeno-almoço.|OHN-tuhyng feesh oo pih-KAY-noo ahl-MOH-soo|Yesterday I made breakfast.}}
<div class="tip">The slip to fix first: <b>Ontem falamos no Porto</b> → <b>Ontem falámos no Porto</b>. The <b>nós</b> past of an <b>-ar</b> verb takes the accent. The line with no accent is the present.</div>
`,
